{ id:'opmcq-c13-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
  stem:'A **72-year-old woman** complains of **gradual central blurring when reading**. Fundus exam shows **multiple yellow deposits beneath the retinal pigment epithelium in the macular area**. What are these deposits called?',
  options:['Cotton wool spots','Hard exudates','Drusen','Lipofuscin'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**The stem is the deck's own definition read backwards.** `L35,36) Chronic visual loss`, on its macular-degeneration slide:\n\n> *\"**Drusen** are **hyaline nodules** (or colloid bodies) **deposited in Bruch's membrane, which separates the inner choroidal vessels from the retinal pigment epithelium**. Drusen maybe small and discrete or larger, with irregular shapes and indistinct edges.\"*\n\nEvery clause of the stem maps onto that sentence. *Beneath the retinal pigment epithelium* is Bruch's membrane, the layer the deck names. *Yellow* is the colour of the hyaline material. *Macular area* is where they matter, because the deck adds that *\"Patients with drusen alone tend to have normal or near normal visual acuity, with minimal metamorphopsia\"* — i.e. drusen are the **early**, still-quiet stage, which is why this patient's complaint is gradual blurring on reading rather than sudden loss.\n\n**Why the location is the whole question.** Three of the four options are retinal deposits and only one of them sits under the RPE. The examiner is testing the layer, not the colour.\n\n| Option | What it actually is | Which layer | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Cotton wool spots** | Micro-infarcts of the **nerve fibre layer** — fluffy, white, ill-defined, and a sign of retinal ischaemia. They appear in this bank's own CRVO vignette four questions later. | Innermost retina, **in front of** the vessels | `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders`; `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` |\n| **B. Hard exudates** | Lipid and protein left behind when leaking capillaries dry out — waxy, yellow, sharply edged, often ringed round a leak. `L19.1` derives them from *\"Loss of pericyte cells between endothelial cells / Leakage of plasma conistitute in the retina (exudate)\"*. ⚠️ **The closest distractor, because they are also yellow** — but they lie in the **outer plexiform layer, within the retina**, not under the RPE, and they belong to a leaking vasculopathy rather than an ageing macula. | Outer retina, above the RPE | `L19.1`, microvascular leakage |\n| **D. Lipofuscin** | A wear-and-tear pigment. Its only appearance in the whole cached ophthalmology corpus is in `L24) Ocular tumors.`, as the orange pigment overlying a choroidal melanoma. It is not the name of a discrete deposit in the macula. | Intracellular, RPE | `L24) Ocular tumors.` — the sole hit for \"lipofuscin\" across all 29 cached decks |\n\n**Where this sits in the disease.** `L35,36` splits the entity into *\"Dry type 90% slow progressive atrophy of RPE and photoreceptors\"* and *\"Wet type 10% RPE detachment and choroidal neovascularization\"*, and treats them differently — *\"For dry AMD dietary supplements of minerals and vitamins especially Vit. E\"*, *\"For wet AMD injections of Anti VEGF\"*. Drusen precede both. Grade Gain keys the same territory from the pathology side at `opqb-t13-537`, where dry AMD is *\"Associated with a disease of the retinal pigment epithelium\"*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Drusen are hyaline (colloid) nodules deposited in Bruch’s membrane between the choriocapillaris and the RPE — the earliest sign of age-related macular degeneration, and the one yellow macular deposit that lies beneath the pigment epithelium rather than within the retina.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.85 (Ch.13 Q1, the first question under the "13. The retina" banner)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'A **premature infant with a history of severe ROP** now presents with a **tractional retinal detachment** on examination. The **retina is pulled forward by fibrous bands, and laser treatment is no longer sufficient**. What is the most appropriate management?',
  options:['Laser photocoagulation','Intravitreal anti-VEGF only','Vitrectomy','Observation'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**The stem has already excluded two of its own options, and the deck supplies the third.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 15 gives tractional detachment exactly one operation:\n\n> **2. Tractional Detachment (Relieving Pull)** — *\"Treatment focuses on removing the mechanical force pulling the retina away: **Vitrectomy & Membrane Peeling** — Surgeons remove the vitreous and carefully **peel away fibrovascular membranes or scar tissue that is pulling on the retina**.\"*\n\nThe stem's *\"fibrous bands\"* are that slide's fibrovascular membranes, and *\"pulled forward\"* is its mechanical force. Once the retina is being physically dragged, no drug and no burn will let it go — the traction has to be cut and delaminated.\n\n**Why this child got here.** `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` treats active ROP with **cryotherapy or laser photocoagulation**, indicated at *\"threshold disease (5 contiguous or 8 non-contiguous clock hours in zone I or zone II, associated with 'plus' disease)\"*, and then prints the result that makes this question possible:\n\n> *\"**75% success · 25% progress to detachment despite treatment**\"*\n\nand names the endpoint on the next slide — *Cicatricial retinopathy of prematurity: temporal vitreoretinal fibrosis and dragging of disc · falciform retinal fold · **total retinal detachment***. **This infant is the deck's own 25 %.**\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Laser photocoagulation** | The stem rules it out in words — *\"laser treatment is no longer sufficient\"*. Laser ablates the ischaemic avascular retina that drives new vessel growth; it does nothing to scar tissue that has already contracted. It is the treatment for the **active** stage this child has passed through. |\n| **B. Intravitreal anti-VEGF only** | Anti-VEGF suppresses the vascular half of a fibrovascular membrane, not the fibrous half. `L20` slide 16 reserves anti-VEGF injections for **exudative** detachment, *\"to stop leaking from abnormal blood vessels\"*. ⚠️ And the word *only* is doing work: giving anti-VEGF while traction is already lifting the retina risks the contraction accelerating as the vascular component involutes — *the crunch phenomenon, **not taken from the course material***. |\n| **D. Observation** | `L20` slide 14 says surgical intervention *\"is almost always required\"* even in the rhegmatogenous case; a tractional detachment in an infant is a permanent loss of the visual system's one developmental window. Nothing in the module offers observation for a detached retina. |\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-ret-vasc` — ROP itself is taught in `L19.2` and this corpus files it there, e.g. `opqb-t16-697` on screening timing. Filed under retinal detachment because what the question tests is the management of a TRACTIONAL detachment, not the staging or screening of ROP.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Once retinopathy of prematurity has progressed to a tractional detachment with fibrovascular bands, laser and anti-VEGF are both too late: the deck’s treatment for tractional detachment is vitrectomy with membrane peeling, to remove the pulling force itself.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.85 (Ch.13 Q2)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
  stem:'A **20-year-old male** patient presented with **gradual diminution of the night vision**. Ophthalmoscopy shows **pale waxy optic disc and attenuated vessels**. Which of the following tests can assist in the diagnosis of this patient?',
  options:['Visual acuity test','Intraocular pressure measurement','Electrophysiological tests','Fundus fluorescein angiography'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**The fundus picture is retinitis pigmentosa and the test is the electroretinogram.** `L21) Optic Nerve` prints the triad in one line — *\"**waxy pale disc + attenuated vessels + peripheral bone-spicule pigmentation**\"* — and this stem quotes two thirds of it.\n\n**Why an electrophysiological test and not a picture.** Retinitis pigmentosa is a disease of the **photoreceptors**, and the earliest cells to fail are the **rods**, which is why the first symptom is night blindness in a young adult whose central acuity is still normal. An ERG measures the mass electrical response of those outer retinal layers directly, so it is abnormal — and diagnostically abnormal — before the fundus looks convincing and before acuity moves. Grade Gain states the principle in its own stem at `opqb-t13-579`: *\"The electroretinogram is useful in assessing the function of the **outer layers of the retina**, especially where the media are opaque\"*, keying **open-angle glaucoma** as the one condition on its list it cannot assess — glaucoma being an inner-retinal, ganglion-cell disease. `opqb-t13-542` prints the same fact from the other side: *\"**ERG can detect early cases**\"* of retinitis pigmentosa.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Visual acuity test** | Measures the fovea, which in early retinitis pigmentosa is the part that still works. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` puts the fovea's *\"high density of cones but no rods\"* — so a rod disease spares exactly what this test measures. It is a measurement, not a diagnosis: it would read normal or near-normal in this patient and exclude nothing. |\n| **B. Intraocular pressure measurement** | Screens for glaucoma. `L35,36` makes tonometry the first step of the chronic-visual-loss workup, so it is not a stupid answer — but it tests aqueous dynamics, and nothing in this stem is glaucomatous. A pale **waxy** disc is not a **cupped** disc; the deck's glaucomatous sign is *\"a cup-disc ratio of 0.5 or more\"*, which the stem does not describe. |\n| **D. Fundus fluorescein angiography** | An angiogram of retinal and choroidal **circulation**. `L35,36` lists it among the tests for macular function and `opqb-t13-551` keys it as the investigation required after a central retinal vein occlusion. Retinitis pigmentosa is a photoreceptor dystrophy, not a vascular disease — the attenuated vessels are a **consequence** of the atrophied retina needing less blood, not the lesion. |\n\n**⚠️ Not the same question as Q18 of this chapter, which describes the same disease.** Q18 gives symptoms and asks for the **diagnosis**; this one gives the fundus and asks for the **test**. The discriminating word is *tests*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'The triad of waxy pale disc, attenuated vessels and night blindness in a young adult is retinitis pigmentosa; because it is a disease of the outer retina, electrophysiology (ERG) is the test that confirms it — and confirms it earlier than acuity, pressure or angiography can.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.85 (Ch.13 Q3)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'A **65-year-old woman** complains of **sudden onset of flashes and floaters**. Visual field examination revealed **peripheral field loss**. She was also found to have **low IOP in the affected eye**. Which of the following is an appropriate line of management for that patient?',
  options:['Anti-VEGF','Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor','Intravitreal steroids','Pneumatic retinopexy'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**Flashes, floaters and a peripheral field defect at 65 are a rhegmatogenous retinal detachment, and this bank keys the one operation on its list.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 14:\n\n> **1. Rhegmatogenous Detachment (Repairing Tears)** — *\"**SURGICAL INTERVENTION IS ALMOST ALWAYS REQUIRED** to seal the retinal break: Vitrectomy ... Scleral Buckling ... **Pneumatic Retinopexy: A GAS BUBBLE is injected into the eye to seal the tear from the inside, often followed by LASER OR CRYOTHERAPY (FREEZING).**\"*\n\nThe symptoms are the deck's own: slide 11 gives rhegmatogenous detachment *\"**Floaters**, flashes of light, and a 'curtain' or shadow in the peripheral vision\"*, onset *\"often sudden and rapid\"*. Slide 6 gives the causes as *\"**Aging (vitreous shrinkage)**, high myopia, or eye trauma\"* — at 65 the ageing vitreous shrinks, separates, tugs, and tears the retina where it stays adherent.\n\n*(The **low intraocular pressure** is the one finding the module does not explain. `L20`'s own not-covered list records *\"low IOP in RD\"* as absent from the deck, and it appears in no other cached ophthalmology file. Mechanistically the detached retina exposes the retinal pigment epithelium to subretinal fluid, which is then reabsorbed faster than aqueous is produced, and the eye softens — a soft eye with flashes and a field defect is a detachment until proved otherwise. **Not taken from the course material.**)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and note that all three are the treatment of something else on this chapter's list:**\n\n| Option | What it actually treats | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Anti-VEGF** | Neovascularisation and leak. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss`: *\"For wet AMD injections of Anti VEGF\"*; `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` uses it for macular oedema; `L20` slide 16 uses it for **exudative** detachment. It cannot close a hole. |\n| **B. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor** | **Raised** intraocular pressure. This patient's pressure is already **low** — the option is not merely useless, it points the wrong way. `L11/L12` file acetazolamide with glaucoma. |\n| **C. Intravitreal steroids** | Inflammation and macular oedema. `L20` slide 16 gives *\"CORTICOSTEROIDS or IMMUNOSUPPRESSANTS for inflammatory conditions like UVEITIS or VKH SYNDROME\"* — the **exudative** column again. Grade Gain keys intravitreal triamcinolone as the treatment NOT used in CRAO at `opqb-t13-524`; this bank makes it the distractor in a detachment. |\n\n**⚠️ The chapter tests the RD triad twice more from different angles** — Q7 asks which cause belongs to the tractional type, Q9 asks which does not belong to the rhegmatogenous one. `L20` slide 9's table is the single sheet that answers all three: *Main Cause* — retinal tear/break, scar tissue pulling, fluid leakage.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Sudden flashes and floaters with peripheral field loss and a soft eye in an older patient is a rhegmatogenous retinal detachment, and it is treated surgically — pneumatic retinopexy, scleral buckling or vitrectomy — never with the injections and pressure-lowering drugs that treat leak, inflammation or ocular hypertension.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.85 (Ch.13 Q4, last question on the page)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
  stem:'A **61-year-old woman** complains of **seeing straight objects as if they were wavy**. History was taken and it showed that the patient was a **heavy smoker**. Ophthalmoscopy revealed **drusen formation**. Which of the following is the most probable diagnosis?',
  options:['Retinal detachment','CRVO','BRVO','Retinitis pigmentosa','AMD'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:"**Two findings each independently name age-related macular degeneration, and together they leave nothing else standing.**\n\n**1. Metamorphopsia.** *\"Straight objects as if they were wavy\"* is the definition of the symptom. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` lists the macular symptom set as *\"Diminution of vision · colour defects · **micropsia** · **macropsia** · **metamorphopsia** · scotoma\"*, and explains why the macula produces it — the macula *\"is composed of both rods and cones and is the area responsible for **detailed, fine central vision**\"*. When fluid or drusen lift and distort the photoreceptor mosaic, the retina's map of straight lines is physically bent, and the patient sees the bend.\n\n**2. Drusen.** The same deck: *\"**Drusen** are hyaline nodules (or colloid bodies) deposited in Bruch's membrane\"* — the hallmark of AMD, and the subject of this chapter's Q1.\n\n**3. The smoking history is not decoration.** `L35,36` divides the risk factors into *\"Risk Factors You CAN'T Control: Age · Race · Gender · Genetics\"* and *\"Risk Factors You CAN Control: **Smoking** · High Blood Pressure · High Cholesterol · Poor Nutrition · UV · Excessive Sugar · Obesity · Sedentary lifestyle\"*. Smoking is printed first among the modifiable ones. The next question in this chapter is built on that list.\n\n**Why the other four are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Retinal detachment** | Wrong tempo and wrong symptom. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` gives rhegmatogenous detachment *\"**Floaters**, flashes of light, and a 'curtain' or shadow in the peripheral vision\"* with onset *\"often sudden and rapid\"*. ⚠️ Detachment **can** distort straight lines once the macula lifts — but it does not produce drusen, and drusen are what the ophthalmoscope actually found. |\n| **B. CRVO** | Acute, painless, and dramatic: the fundus of a central retinal vein occlusion is this bank's own *\"blood and thunder appearance ... with cotton wool spots and diffuse retinal hemorrhages\"* (Q12 of this chapter). Nothing of that is described here, and this woman's complaint is gradual. |\n| **C. BRVO** | Same objection, restricted to one quadrant — so the field defect is sectoral, not central, and again there are haemorrhages rather than drusen. |\n| **D. Retinitis pigmentosa** | A **rod** dystrophy: night blindness first, then peripheral field, with `L21) Optic Nerve`'s triad of *\"waxy pale disc + attenuated vessels + peripheral bone-spicule pigmentation\"*. It attacks the periphery and spares the centre until late — the exact opposite of this presentation. It is the keyed answer at Q15 and Q18 of this chapter, where the findings are the triad. |\n\n**⚠️ This is the antecedent of Q6**, which asks which of these history items is a predisposing factor. Grade Gain covers the same disease from the pathology side at `opqb-t13-537` (dry AMD as a disease of the retinal pigment epithelium) and from the prognosis side at `opqb-t13-559` (senile macular degeneration as permanent visual loss).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Metamorphopsia plus drusen in an elderly smoker is age-related macular degeneration; distortion of straight lines localises the lesion to the macula, and drusen date it to the degenerative rather than the vascular or dystrophic diseases.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.86 (Ch.13 Q5)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
  stem:'A **61-year-old woman**, a **heavy smoker**, complains of **seeing straight objects as if they were wavy**; ophthalmoscopy revealed **drusen formation**. Which of the following is a predisposing factor for the patient\'s condition?',
  options:['Old age','Female gender','Smoking','A & B','All of the above'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:"*(⚠️ **Back-reference repaired.** The bank prints only *\"In the previous case, which of the following is a predisposing factor for the patient's condition?\"*. Its antecedent is `opmcq-c13-5`, the question printed immediately above it on book p.86, and because the app hashes question order from the id, that question is never the one on the screen before this. The findings restated above are the antecedent's own printed words and nothing else. They do not give the key away: the stem supplies age, sex and smoking as **history**, and the examinee must still decide that each of the three counts as a risk factor.)*\n\n**The key is E because `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` prints all three on its risk-factor slide**, split into two columns:\n\n> **Risk Factors You CAN'T Control:** *\"**Age** · Race · **Gender** · Genetics\"*\n> **Risk Factors You CAN Control:** *\"**Smoking** · High Blood Pressure · High Cholesterol · Poor Nutrition · UV · Excessive Sugar · Obesity · Sedentary lifestyle\"*\n\nSo old age (A), female gender (B) and smoking (C) are each on the deck's own list, which makes **E. All of the above** the only option that does not leave one of them out.\n\n**Why D is the trap and not the answer.** \"A & B\" collects the two **non-modifiable** factors and drops the one modifiable factor in the stem — and smoking is the risk factor the deck puts **first** among those the patient can do something about. A candidate who reads the two columns as \"unavoidable versus lifestyle\" and answers with the unavoidable pair has understood the slide's layout and missed its point. The whole clinical value of the risk list is that one item on it can be acted on.\n\n| Option | Status | Where the deck prints it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Old age** | Correct, but incomplete | *Age*, first item under \"CAN'T Control\"; the disease is named **age-related** macular degeneration |\n| **B. Female gender** | Correct, but incomplete | *Gender*, third item under \"CAN'T Control\" |\n| **C. Smoking** | Correct, but incomplete | *Smoking*, first item under \"CAN Control\" |\n| **D. A & B** | Two of three | Omits the modifiable factor |\n\n*(The deck lists **Gender** without saying which sex carries the higher risk. Large population studies find AMD somewhat more common in women, in part because women live longer; this is **not taken from the course material** and is not needed to answer — the slide names gender as a factor, and the bank asks only whether it is one.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The one sentence that matters in the clinic is that stopping smoking is the only item on this list a patient can change, and `L35,36` backs it up with treatment that differs by type — *\"For dry AMD dietary supplements of minerals and vitamins especially Vit. E\"* and *\"For wet AMD injections of Anti VEGF\"*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'The deck\'s AMD risk list runs across both of its columns — age, race, gender and genetics on the unmodifiable side, smoking first on the modifiable side — so age, female sex and smoking are all predisposing factors, and an answer that keeps only the unmodifiable pair drops the one factor the patient can change.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.86 (Ch.13 Q6; printed as "In the previous case…", antecedent Q5 on the same page)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'Which of the following is a well-known cause of **tractional** retinal detachment?',
  options:['Malignant hypertension','Posterior uveitis','High myopia','Sickle cell disease'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**`L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` names sickle cell disease in the tractional list and nowhere else.** Slide 7:\n\n> **Tractional Retinal Detachment (TRD)** — *\"The retina is **pulled away** from the underlying tissue by **scar tissue or membranes** growing on its surface. **UNLIKE RRD, THERE IS TYPICALLY NO INITIAL TEAR.** Common Causes: **Advanced diabetic retinopathy (THE MOST COMMON CAUSE), sickle cell disease, or previous ocular trauma.**\"*\n\n**Why sickle cell belongs there — and the module stages it for you.** `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives *\"Staging of proliferative sickle-cell retinopathy\"* its own slide, and the five stages ARE the mechanism, ending in the very lesion this question asks about:\n\n> *\"1 - Peripheral arteriolar occlusion · 2 - Peripheral arteriovenous anastomoses · 3 - Neovascularization ('**sea-fan**') · 4 - Vitreous haemorrhage · 5 - **Fibrovascular proliferation and traction**\"*\n\nSickled cells occlude the peripheral retinal capillaries (stage 1); the ischaemic retina drives new vessel growth (stage 3); those fronds are fibrovascular, and fibrous tissue contracts (stage 5). The final common path is identical to proliferative diabetic retinopathy — a membrane on the retinal surface that shortens and lifts the retina off. `L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases` prints the same three photographs and adds the counter-intuitive epidemiology: sickle retinopathy is *\"More common to cause ocular complications with sickle **hemoglobin C (SC)** and **sickle cell thalassemia (S Thal)** disease than patients with sickle cell (SS) disease.\"*\n\n**⚠️ Every distractor is a genuine cause of retinal detachment — of the wrong TYPE.** That is the whole design of the question, and `L20`'s slide 9 table is the sheet that sorts them:\n\n| Option | Which type it causes | The deck's own words |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Malignant hypertension** | **Exudative** | Slide 8, ERD: *\"Common Causes: Inflammatory diseases (like uveitis), tumors (such as choroidal melanoma), or systemic conditions like **severe hypertension**\"* — fluid leaks from damaged choroidal vessels and pools beneath an intact retina |\n| **B. Posterior uveitis** | **Exudative** | Same slide: *\"**Inflammatory diseases (like uveitis)**\"*. Slide 16 treats it accordingly: *\"CORTICOSTEROIDS or IMMUNOSUPPRESSANTS for inflammatory conditions like UVEITIS or VKH SYNDROME\"* |\n| **C. High myopia** | **Rhegmatogenous** | Slide 6, RRD: *\"Common Causes: Aging (vitreous shrinkage), **high myopia (nearsightedness)**, or eye trauma\"* — the stretched globe thins the peripheral retina until it tears |\n\n**The one-line discriminator**, from slide 9's table: **Main Cause** — *retinal tear or break* (RRD) · *scar tissue pulling* (TRD) · *fluid leakage* (ERD); **Retinal Surface** — *corrugated / wrinkled* · *smooth / taut* · *smooth / bullous*. A taut, smooth, tented retina without a break is tractional.\n\n**⚠️ Note that ocular trauma appears in TWO of the three lists** — as a cause of RRD on slide 6 and of TRD on slide 7. It is not a discriminator on its own, which is why this question uses sickle cell instead. Grade Gain keys the commonest cause of the tractional type at `opqb-t13-526` (*\"Diabetes is the most common cause\"*), and this chapter's Q17 tests the same pairing from the diabetic side.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Tractional detachment is caused by contracting fibrovascular membranes, so its causes are the proliferative retinopathies — advanced diabetic retinopathy above all, then sickle cell disease and previous trauma — while malignant hypertension and posterior uveitis cause exudative detachment and high myopia causes the rhegmatogenous type.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.86 (Ch.13 Q7)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
  stem:'Which of the following increases the risk of **central retinal vein occlusion**?',
  options:['Beta blockers','Steroids','Oral contraceptives','Anemia'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**A vein thromboses when the blood in it is more likely to clot, and the combined oral contraceptive pill is the commonest reason a young woman's blood is.** `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` lists the predisposing factors for retinal vein occlusion as *\"increasing age · hypertension · diabetes · **coagulation abnormalities** · raised intraocular pressure · periphlebitis\"* — and the oestrogen-containing pill is a coagulation abnormality: it raises fibrinogen and factors VII and X and lowers protein S and antithrombin, shifting the balance towards thrombosis. It is why CRVO is one of the few retinal vascular occlusions that turns up in a patient under 40 with an otherwise clean history.\n\n*(⚠️ **A measured gap.** Grepped case-insensitively, **\"oral contracept\" returns ZERO hits across all twenty-nine cached ophthalmology lecture files**; the only hit anywhere under `content\\ophtho` is in `book\\ch04-lacrimal-system.txt`, in an unrelated context. The deck names *coagulation abnormalities* generically and stops. The specific mechanism above is therefore **not taken from the course material** — the link between the combined pill and venous thrombosis is standard and uncontested, and the bank asks for it directly.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and two of them are traps built out of real ophthalmology:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Beta blockers** | ⚠️ **The best trap on the list, because topical beta blockers are ophthalmic drugs.** Timolol is a first-line glaucoma medication, and *raised intraocular pressure* is on `L19.2`'s own CRVO risk list — so the reflex is to connect them. But beta blockers **lower** intraocular pressure, which moves the risk in the protective direction. They have no prothrombotic effect. |\n| **B. Steroids** | Steroids raise intraocular pressure in responders, which touches one item on the risk list — but they are also, in this same module, part of the **treatment** of the macular oedema that follows a vein occlusion. `L20` slide 16 uses *\"CORTICOSTEROIDS or IMMUNOSUPPRESSANTS\"* for exudative disease. Not a recognised cause of CRVO. |\n| **D. Anemia** | Anaemia **lowers** blood viscosity. The haematological conditions that predispose to vein occlusion are the ones that thicken blood or over-produce cells — **polycythaemia**, hyperviscosity syndromes, myeloma, the thrombophilias. Severe anaemia has its own retinopathy of haemorrhages and cotton wool spots, but it is not a cause of venous occlusion. |\n\n**⚠️ Read the question's vessel before answering.** This chapter runs the vein and the artery side by side and keys them differently: Q19 asks the commonest cause of **CRVO / BRVO** and keys **thrombosis**; Q20 asks the commonest cause of **CRAO / BRAO** and keys **embolism**. Grade Gain tests the same risk list negatively at `opqb-t13-578`, where hypertension, open-angle glaucoma and diabetes are all predisposing and **hypermature cataract** is the odd one out.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A retinal vein occlusion in a patient under fifty is a haematological question, not an ophthalmic one: stop the pill, and screen for thrombophilia, hyperviscosity and vasculitis rather than assuming atherosclerosis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Central retinal vein occlusion is a thrombotic event, so its risk factors are the prothrombotic ones — the deck\'s "coagulation abnormalities", of which the combined oral contraceptive pill is the commonest in a young patient — while beta blockers lower IOP, steroids treat the sequel, and anaemia thins rather than thickens the blood.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.86 (Ch.13 Q8)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'All of the following are among the causes of **rhegmatogenous** retinal detachment **except**?',
  options:['Posterior vitreous detachment','Degenerative retinal changes','Trauma','Diabetic retinopathy'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"***Rhegma*** **is Greek for a break, and that is the whole question: the rhegmatogenous causes are the ones that tear the retina.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 6:\n\n> **Rhegmatogenous Retinal Detachment (RRD)** — *\"The **MOST COMMON** type. It occurs when a **physical TEAR OR BREAK** forms in the retina, allowing vitreous fluid to seep underneath and lift it off the wall of the eye. Common Causes: **Aging (vitreous shrinkage), high myopia (nearsightedness), or eye trauma.**\"*\n\n**Diabetic retinopathy is the exception because it detaches the retina by pulling, not by tearing.** The same deck, one slide later: TRD is caused by *\"scar tissue or membranes growing on its surface... **UNLIKE RRD, THERE IS TYPICALLY NO INITIAL TEAR.** Common Causes: **Advanced diabetic retinopathy (THE MOST COMMON CAUSE)**\"* — and slide 10's figure labels that cause **proliferative** diabetic retinopathy, which is the stage at which fibrovascular tissue exists to contract. Grade Gain prints the same assignment at `opqb-t13-526`: tractional detachment, *\"Diabetes is the most common cause\"*.\n\n**Why the other three do belong to the rhegmatogenous list:**\n\n| Option | The break it makes |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Posterior vitreous detachment** | The deck's *\"aging (vitreous shrinkage)\"* named precisely. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 spells out the chain: *\"The vitreous humour degenerates and condenses (**vitreous syneresis**) which predisposes to the separation of the vitreous from its attachments to the retina and optic disc, called a **posterior vitreous detachment (PVD)**. This can predispose the patient to **retinal traction, retinal tears**, and, in the worst-case scenario, **retinal detachment**.\"* Where the vitreous stays stuck as it pulls away, it takes a piece of retina with it. |\n| **B. Degenerative retinal changes** | Peripheral degenerations — lattice above all — thin the retina until it holes. This is the second half of the deck's own title, *\"Retinal detachment **and degenerations**\"*, and high myopia acts through the same route. |\n| **C. Trauma** | Printed verbatim in slide 6's cause list as *\"eye trauma\"*. ⚠️ Trauma appears in the **tractional** list too (*\"previous ocular trauma\"*), so it is not a discriminator on its own — but it is unambiguously a rhegmatogenous cause and therefore not the exception. |\n\n**⚠️ A note on how this question is printed.** The stem and options A–C sit at the foot of book p.86; **option D — which is the key — and the answer line are at the head of p.87**. An orphaned key is easy to mis-transcribe as a missing option; both halves were read before this entry was written.\n\n**⚠️ And note that PVD is on two decks' own \"not covered\" lists** (`L20` and `L31,32`) while the **book** names and explains it at p.17, p.41 and p.42. Two absences recorded against two decks are not an absence from the module.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Rhegmatogenous detachment requires a retinal break, so its causes are the tearing ones — posterior vitreous detachment, peripheral degeneration and trauma; diabetic retinopathy detaches the retina by fibrovascular traction with no initial tear, which makes it the exception here and the commonest cause of the tractional type.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.86-87 (Ch.13 Q9; stem and options A-C on p.86, option D and the answer line on p.87)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
  stem:'A **65-year-old man** presents with **sudden painless severe monocular vision loss**. Ophthalmoscopic examination revealed **cherry red spots together with venous stasis**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['CRAO','CRVO','Retinal detachment','AMD','Retinitis pigmentosa'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"**The cherry-red spot is the sign, and `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints it in exactly one place — the CRAO slide:**\n\n> **Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO)** — *\"VA < 6/60 · APD - marked · **Retinal whitening** · **'Cherry-red spot' at macula** · **Arteriolar and venular narrowing** · **Sludging and segmentation of blood column (cattle-trucking)** · Very poor prognosis\"*\n\n`L31,32) Acute visual Loss` builds its first case on the same finding — *\"Retinal examination reveals a **white, opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot**\"*, diagnosis *\"Central Retinal Artery Occlusion\"*.\n\n**Why the spot is red at all**, since nothing about this is haemorrhagic: the inner retina, fed by the central retinal artery, goes opaque and white when it infarcts. The **fovea** has no inner retinal layers to swell — `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` notes its *\"high density of cones but no rods\"* and Grade Gain adds at `opqb-t13-546` that the fovea is nourished by the **choriocapillaris**, not the retinal circulation. So the fovea stays thin and transparent while everything around it turns white, and the intact orange choroid shows through it as a red centre in a white field. It is not a lesion; it is a window.\n\n**⚠️ A defect in the stem, recorded and not disputed.** *\"Venous stasis\"* reads at first like a **CRVO** phrase, and \"venous stasis retinopathy\" is a name that has been used for non-ischaemic vein occlusion. It looks like a word from the wrong disease sitting next to the right sign. **It is not, quite:** the deck's own CRAO list includes *\"arteriolar and **venular** narrowing\"* and *\"**sludging and segmentation of the blood column** (cattle-trucking)\"* — which is stasis in the retinal veins, and is one of the classic bedside signs of an arterial occlusion, because blood that is not arriving does not move on. The stem is loosely worded rather than wrong, and the printed key stands.\n\n**Why the other four are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **B. CRVO** | ⚠️ The distractor the stem's wording flirts with. But a vein occlusion is a **haemorrhagic** fundus — this bank's own Q12 gives it as *\"blood and thunder appearance ... cotton wool spots and diffuse retinal hemorrhages\"*, and `L19.2`'s ischaemic-CRVO slide lists *\"marked venous tortuosity and engorgement · extensive retinal haemorrhages · severe disc oedema\"*. **No cherry-red spot.** Grade Gain keys precisely that at `opqb-t13-514`, where every listed CRVO feature is correct **except** *\"cherry red spot\"*. |\n| **C. Retinal detachment** | Sudden, yes — but the symptoms are flashes, floaters and a peripheral curtain (`L20`), and the retina looks corrugated and mobile, not white with a red centre. |\n| **D. AMD** | Gradual central blurring with drusen and metamorphopsia (Q1 and Q5 of this chapter). Not sudden, not painless-catastrophic, not white. |\n| **E. Retinitis pigmentosa** | Years of night blindness with a waxy disc, attenuated vessels and bone spicules (Q15, Q18). A different clock entirely. |\n\n**⚠️ Not a fold with Q26 of this chapter**, which is also sudden painless monocular loss keyed to CRAO. Q26 prints *\"relative afferent pupillary defect\"* where this one prints *\"venous stasis\"*, and its four-option menu runs CRVO / optic neuritis / CRAO / BRAO against this one's five. Two questions.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'A cherry-red spot on a whitened retina with sudden painless monocular loss is central retinal artery occlusion: the infarcted inner retina goes opaque everywhere except the fovea, which has no inner layers and is fed by the choroid, so the choroidal red shows through the one clear window.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.87 (Ch.13 Q10)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
  stem:'A **65-year-old man** presented with **sudden painless severe monocular vision loss**, and ophthalmoscopy revealed **cherry red spots together with venous stasis**. Which of the following is a medication prescribed by the ophthalmologist in order to control that acute condition?',
  options:['IV mannitol 20%','Systemic steroids','Topical miotics','All of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"*(⚠️ **Back-reference repaired.** The bank prints only *\"In the previous case, which of the following is a medication prescribed by the ophthalmologist in order to control that acute condition?\"*. Its antecedent is `opmcq-c13-10`, printed immediately above it on book p.87; the app hashes question order, so that question is never the one on screen before this. The findings restated above are the antecedent's printed words and nothing more — the diagnosis is deliberately **not** named, because the printed stem does not name it either.)*\n\n**The logic of the drug is the logic of the occlusion.** Perfusion through the central retinal artery depends on the difference between the arterial pressure driving blood in and the **intraocular pressure** pushing back on the vessel. Nothing can dissolve the embolus at the bedside, so every emergency manoeuvre attacks the other side of that equation: drop the intraocular pressure sharply, widen the gradient, and hope the embolus is pushed distally into a smaller branch where it costs less retina. **IV mannitol 20 %** is the fastest way to drop it — an osmotic agent that pulls water out of the vitreous within minutes.\n\n*(⚠️ **A measured gap, and it is total.** `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` builds its Case 1 on this exact presentation and then prints a slide headed **\"Management\"** with **nothing on it** — one word on an otherwise empty page. That deck's own not-covered list names what is missing: *\"CRAO: ocular massage, anterior chamber paracentesis, IOP-lowering drops or acetazolamide, rebreathing/carbogen, the 'retina survives ~90 minutes' style time window, thrombolysis\"*. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives CRAO its signs, its causes and its carotid imaging, but no treatment slide either. **The mechanism above is therefore not taken from the course material.** What the module does supply is the drug class: `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lists **\"Hyperosmotic agents — IV mannitol · Oral hypertonic glycerol\"** among the IOP-lowering treatments of acute angle-closure glaucoma, so mannitol is taught here as the emergency IOP-lowering drug. This question borrows it for the other emergency in which IOP must fall fast.)*\n\n**Grade Gain fills the same gap from the other direction.** `opqb-t13-524` asks which treatment is **not** used in CRAO and keys **intravitreal triamcinolone** — leaving *ocular massage*, *5 % CO₂ inhalation* and *anterior chamber paracentesis* standing as the ones that are. Every one of those three is an IOP-lowering or vasodilating manoeuvre. The two banks agree on the principle even though neither deck prints it.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **B. Systemic steroids** | ⚠️ **The one option with a real clinical case behind it, and it is still not the answer here.** Where an artery occlusion is **arteritic** — giant cell arteritis — high-dose systemic steroids are urgent, to save the fellow eye. `L19.2` does list *periarteritis* under vaso-obliteration (*\"SLE may cause branch artery occlusion\"*). But this patient has no headache, no jaw claudication, no scalp tenderness and no temporal artery signs, and steroids do nothing to an embolus. The bank asks for the medication that controls **the acute condition described**. |\n| **C. Topical miotics** | Pilocarpine appears on `L12`'s acute angle-closure list, and that is the whole trap: three of these four options are lifted from the treatment of a different ocular emergency. A miotic constricts the pupil to pull the iris out of the angle — irrelevant to a blocked artery, and it lowers IOP too slowly and too little to matter. |\n| **D. All of the above** | Fails on both B and C. |\n\n**⚠️ Note this question prints only four options and ends at D.** Its antecedent prints five.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** CRAO is a stroke of the eye and the clock is the treatment: lower the pressure by whatever route is fastest — massage, mannitol, acetazolamide, paracentesis — while simultaneously asking the two questions that change the patient's life rather than the eye, namely *is this giant cell arteritis* (ESR, CRP) and *where is the embolus from* (carotid duplex, which `L19.2` devotes four slides to, and cardiac assessment).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Nothing dissolves a retinal embolus acutely, so treatment attacks the other side of the perfusion equation — drop the intraocular pressure fast, with IV mannitol, acetazolamide, ocular massage or paracentesis, to widen the gradient and drive the embolus distally.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.87 (Ch.13 Q11; printed as "In the previous case…", antecedent Q10 on the same page)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
  stem:'A **57-year-old diabetic and hypertensive male** patient complained of **acute painless loss of vision** in his left eye. Ophthalmoscopy revealed **(blood and thunder appearance) together with cotton wool spots and diffuse retinal hemorrhages**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['CRAO','CRVO','Retinal detachment','Retinitis pigmentosa','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"*(⚠️ **The bank misprints the keyed option.** Option B is printed **\"CVRO\"** — a letter transposition for **CRVO**, verified by re-rendering the page at 2.5×. It is corrected above; the key does not move, because B is B whatever it is spelled. Recorded per the standing rule that a source fault is repaired in the entry and reported, never silently overwritten.)*\n\n**\"Blood and thunder\" is the fundus of a central retinal vein occlusion and of nothing else.** When the vein blocks at the lamina cribrosa, the whole retinal venous outflow backs up at once: veins engorge and become tortuous, capillaries burst in every quadrant, and the retina fills with haemorrhage from disc to periphery. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders`, on the ischaemic CRVO slide:\n\n> *\"VA < 6/60 · APD - marked · **Marked venous tortuosity and engorgement** · **Extensive retinal haemorrhages** · Variable cotton wool spots · Severe disc oedema · Macular ischaemia · **Very poor prognosis** · **Rubeosis irides in 50 %**\"*\n\nEvery element of the stem is on that list. The **cotton wool spots** are the deck's *\"variable cotton wool spots\"* — micro-infarcts of the nerve fibre layer, the sign that the occlusion is ischaemic rather than merely congestive. The deck's non-ischaemic slide, by contrast, gives *\"VA > CF · APD - mild · mild venous tortuosity and dilatation · **mild to moderate** retinal haemorrhages\"*. This patient's fundus is the severe one.\n\n**Why his history was given.** `L19.2` lists the predisposing factors as *\"increasing age · **hypertension** · **diabetes** · coagulation abnormalities · raised intraocular pressure · periphlebitis\"*, and this man is 57 with both of the first two. Grade Gain keys the same list negatively at `opqb-t13-578`, where hypertension, open-angle glaucoma and diabetes all predispose and **hypermature cataract** is the exception.\n\n**Why the other four are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. CRAO** | Also sudden and painless, and the closest distractor — but an **arterial** occlusion produces a **white** retina with a cherry-red spot, arteriolar narrowing and cattle-trucking (`L19.2`). It is the pale fundus; this is the red one. The two are keyed against each other three times in this chapter. |\n| **C. Retinal detachment** | Flashes, floaters and a peripheral curtain (`L20`), not a haemorrhagic fundus. |\n| **D. Retinitis pigmentosa** | Gradual night blindness with a waxy disc and bone spicules — years, not hours. |\n| **E. None of the above** | Only if B were not on the list. \"None of the above\" is printed several times in this chapter and is **never** the key. |\n\n**⚠️ This is the antecedent of both Q13 and Q14**, which ask for its predisposing factor and its expected complication. Q14 is reached through Q13, which is itself a back-reference with no case of its own.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'A blood-and-thunder fundus — engorged tortuous veins, haemorrhages in all four quadrants, cotton wool spots and disc oedema — is an ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion, the haemorrhagic counterpart to the white retina and cherry-red spot of arterial occlusion.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.87 (Ch.13 Q12; option B printed "CVRO")' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
  stem:'A **57-year-old diabetic and hypertensive male** patient had **acute painless loss of vision** in his left eye, with **(blood and thunder appearance) together with cotton wool spots and diffuse retinal hemorrhages** on ophthalmoscopy. Which of the following is a predisposing factor?',
  options:['DM','HTN','Male gender','A & B','All of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"*(⚠️⚠️ **Back-reference repaired, and this is the one the rule exists for — reported, not smoothed over.** The bank prints only *\"In the previous case, which of the following is a predisposing factor?\"*, and its antecedent is `opmcq-c13-12` on the same page. **That antecedent's very first printed clause is \"diabetic and hypertensive\" — which is options A and B, and A & B is the key.** Restating the antecedent therefore hands over most of the discrimination. It is restated anyway, for three reasons: it is what the book prints; cutting the comorbidities would leave the question with no case at all and make it unanswerable; and what the item actually tests is which of the printed comorbidities counts, so the examinee must still reject **male gender** and must still decline **All of the above**. The alternative — inventing a case — would be worse. **Hand-checked against the printed key after writing.**)*\n\n**Both diabetes and hypertension are on the deck's list; male sex is not.** `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives the predisposing factors for retinal vein occlusion as:\n\n> *\"increasing age · **hypertension** · **diabetes** · coagulation abnormalities · raised intraocular pressure · periphlebitis\"*\n\n**Why these two and why they act differently.** A vein thromboses where it is squeezed. The central retinal artery and vein share a common adventitial sheath as they pass through the lamina cribrosa, so a **stiff, thickened, hypertensive artery compresses the vein against the rigid scleral canal**, turbulence follows, and the vein clots at exactly that point. **Diabetes** contributes from the blood side rather than the wall side — endothelial dysfunction, raised plasma viscosity and increased platelet aggregability. *(The anatomical mechanism of the shared adventitial sheath is **not taken from the course material**; the deck names the risk factors without explaining them.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. DM alone** | True but incomplete — it drops hypertension, which the deck lists **before** diabetes. |\n| **B. HTN alone** | True but incomplete for the same reason. Hypertension is generally the single most important systemic association of vein occlusion, but the question offers a combined option. |\n| **C. Male gender** | ⚠️ **Printed in the antecedent's very first line and deliberately not on the deck's list.** Retinal vein occlusion has no meaningful sex predilection; its risk is carried by age and by vascular comorbidity. This is the option the item is really testing — the trap is to treat every clause of a vignette as a risk factor because it was written down. |\n| **E. All of the above** | Fails on C. |\n\n**⚠️ Contrast with Q6 of this chapter**, where the parallel option — *All of the above* — **is** the key, because gender genuinely appears on the AMD risk slide (*\"Age · Race · **Gender** · Genetics\"*). The two questions have the same shape and opposite answers, and the difference is entirely in what the deck happens to list. **A key letter is never the thing to memorise.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Hypertension and diabetes are both printed predisposing factors for retinal vein occlusion — hypertension by compressing the vein against its shared sheath at the lamina cribrosa, diabetes through the blood itself — while male sex is not a risk factor, however prominently a vignette states it.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.87 (Ch.13 Q13; printed as "In the previous case…", antecedent Q12 on the same page)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
  stem:'A **57-year-old diabetic and hypertensive male** patient had **acute painless loss of vision** in his left eye, with **(blood and thunder appearance) together with cotton wool spots and diffuse retinal hemorrhages** on ophthalmoscopy. Which of the following is an expected complication?',
  options:['Glaucoma','Vitreous hemorrhage','Macular edema','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"*(⚠️ **Back-reference repaired, through a chain.** The bank prints *\"In the previous case, which of the following is an expected complication?\"* — but the question immediately above it, `opmcq-c13-13`, is itself a back-reference with no case of its own, so the chain resolves back to `opmcq-c13-12` on the previous page. Its printed findings are restated above and nothing else; no complication is named in the stem.)*\n\n**All three complications follow from one event: the ischaemic retina starts making VEGF.** An ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion leaves large areas of retina without perfusion; those areas signal for new vessels; and new vessels are both leaky and fragile, wherever they grow. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` names each consequence on its own slide.\n\n| Complication | Where the deck prints it | How it happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Glaucoma** | Ischaemic CRVO signs: ***\"Rubeosis irides in 50 %\"***; and its management slide — *\"Check every month for 6 months · **Look for rubeosis and angle new vessels** · Treat neovascularization by **panretinal photocoagulation**\"* | New vessels grow on the iris and into the drainage angle, where the fibrovascular membrane blocks and then contracts the angle shut — **neovascular glaucoma**. Half of ischaemic occlusions do this, which is why the deck sets a monthly review for six months |\n| **B. Vitreous hemorrhage** | The deck's proliferative-retinopathy staging (sickle-cell slide) prints the sequence *\"3 - Neovascularization ('sea-fan') → **4 - Vitreous haemorrhage** → 5 - Fibrovascular proliferation and traction\"* — the same path any ischaemic retina follows | Retinal or disc new vessels have no supporting structure and bleed into the vitreous |\n| **C. Macular edema** | Printed as a complication of vein occlusion twice: on the non-ischaemic CRVO slide (***\"Chronic macular oedema\"***) and for branch occlusion — *\"**Complications - chronic macular oedema** and neovascularization\"*, with a whole slide on *\"Management of chronic macular oedema\"* opening ***\"Most common cause of persistent poor VA\"*** | The blocked vein raises capillary hydrostatic pressure and fluid is forced into the macula |\n\nSo **D. All of the above** is the only option that accounts for the retina, the vitreous and the anterior segment together — and the question is really asking whether the candidate knows that a vein occlusion is not finished when the haemorrhages fade.\n\n**Why E is wrong:** all three are printed complications, so *None of the above* fails on each.\n\n**⚠️ The clinically important asymmetry.** Macular oedema is the commonest cause of **persistent poor vision** — the deck says so in those words — but **neovascular glaucoma is the one that costs the eye**, and it typically arrives around three months after an ischaemic occlusion. That is why the deck's management is a **calendar** rather than a prescription: monthly review for six months, looking specifically at the iris and the angle, with panretinal photocoagulation the moment new vessels appear.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'An ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion drives VEGF release, and its complications follow the new vessels wherever they grow: macular oedema as the commonest cause of persistent poor vision, vitreous haemorrhage from fragile retinal new vessels, and neovascular glaucoma from rubeosis in half of ischaemic cases.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.88 (Ch.13 Q14, first question on the page; printed as "In the previous case…", chain resolves through Q13 to Q12 on p.87)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
  stem:'Ophthalmoscopy was carried out for a **26-year-old man** who was complaining of **diminished vision**, and it showed a triad of **(bone spicules, attenuated vessels and waxy disc)**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Retinal detachment','CRVO','BRVO','Retinitis pigmentosa'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**The stem prints the module's triad word for word.** `L21) Optic Nerve` gives it in a single line — ***\"waxy pale disc + attenuated vessels + peripheral bone-spicule pigmentation\"*** — and this question quotes all three elements, in a different order.\n\n**Each sign is a different consequence of the same photoreceptor death:**\n\n| Sign | What produces it |\n|---|---|\n| **Bone spicules** | As the outer retina atrophies, retinal pigment epithelial cells migrate inward and settle along the perivascular spaces of the mid-peripheral retina, taking the branching shape that gives them their name. The pigment is not new — it has moved |\n| **Attenuated vessels** | An atrophic retina has almost no metabolic demand, so the retinal arterioles narrow to match. This is **secondary**, not a vascular disease: the vessels are thin because the tissue they feed is gone |\n| **Waxy pale disc** | **Consecutive optic atrophy.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.194 defines the term precisely: *\"3. **Consecutive optic atrophy**: Which occurs due to **retinal diseases e.g., Retinitis pigmentosa** / degenerative myopia. **Retinal examination shows the primary cause of the optic atrophy**\"* — the ganglion cells die because the photoreceptors that drove them died first |\n\nSo the pale disc here is **not** a primary optic neuropathy, and the narrow vessels are **not** a primary vasculopathy. Both are downstream of the retina, which is why the book tells you to look at the retina to find the cause.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Retinal detachment** | The detached retina is corrugated, mobile and elevated, with a visible break; `L20` gives the symptoms as flashes, floaters and a peripheral curtain of sudden onset. None of the triad appears. ⚠️ RP and detachment are linked at one remove — both cause visual loss in a young adult, and high myopia predisposes to detachment — but the fundus is unmistakable. |\n| **B. CRVO** | The haemorrhagic fundus of Q12: engorged **tortuous** veins, extensive haemorrhages, cotton wool spots, disc **oedema**. Every one of those is the opposite of this fundus, which is pale, dry and attenuated. And it is acute. |\n| **C. BRVO** | Same objections in one sector only. `L19.2` gives its complications as *\"chronic macular oedema and neovascularization\"*, with *\"vascular sheathing and collaterals\"* and hard exudates in old cases — sheathing is a **white line along a vessel**, which is the closest any distractor comes to \"attenuated\", and is still a different thing. |\n\n**Where this fits the syllabus.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.185's table of visual-loss causes files retinitis pigmentosa under **chronic (weeks to months), posterior segment**, beside AMD and diabetic retinopathy — not among the sudden or acute causes. `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` lists it among the causes of blindness, and `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` names it as a cause of **complicated cataract**, which is what Q27 of this chapter tests.\n\n**⚠️ This is the antecedent of Q16**, which asks for the treatment. **⚠️ And it is not a fold with Q18**, which describes the same disease by **symptoms** rather than by fundus signs: the discriminating token is *\"bone spicules\"*, printed only here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'The triad of peripheral bone-spicule pigmentation, attenuated vessels and a waxy pale disc is retinitis pigmentosa — and all three are secondary to photoreceptor death: migrated RPE pigment, vessels narrowed to match a retina that no longer demands blood, and consecutive optic atrophy.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.88 (Ch.13 Q15)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
  stem:'A **26-year-old man** complaining of **diminished vision** was found on ophthalmoscopy to have a triad of **(bone spicules, attenuated vessels and waxy disc)**. Which of the following is the best line of treatment?',
  options:['Anti-VEGF','PRP','Steroids','Surgery','No available treatment'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:"*(⚠️ **Back-reference repaired.** The bank prints only *\"In the previous case, which of the following is the best line of treatment?\"*; the antecedent is `opmcq-c13-15` on the same page and its printed triad is restated above. The repair carries the findings only and does not name the disease — and naming findings cannot reveal that no treatment exists, so the item is unaffected.)*\n\n**The disease is retinitis pigmentosa, and the honest answer is that nothing on this list changes its course.** It is an inherited **photoreceptor dystrophy**: rods die first, then cones, over decades, driven by a mutation. There is no vessel to seal, no inflammation to suppress and no tissue to reattach — the cells are simply going, and none of the four active options addresses that.\n\n*(⚠️ **A measured gap, and the absence is real.** Searched case-insensitively across all twenty-nine cached ophthalmology lecture files and all twelve cached book chapters: **no source in this module states that retinitis pigmentosa is untreatable, or offers any treatment for it.** `L21) Optic Nerve` prints the fundus triad and stops. `L1,2)` lists RP among causes of blindness. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` names it only as a cause of **complicated cataract**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.185 files it under chronic posterior-segment visual loss, and p.194 uses it as an example of consecutive optic atrophy. **The material teaches the diagnosis and never the management** — everything in this explanation about what to do next is therefore **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**Why each of the four active options is wrong — and each is the right treatment for a different disease in this same chapter:**\n\n| Option | What it actually treats, and why it fails here |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Anti-VEGF** | Suppresses **neovascularisation and leak**: `L35,36` — *\"For wet AMD injections of Anti VEGF\"*; `L20` uses it for exudative detachment. RP has no neovascular component. |\n| **B. PRP** | Panretinal photocoagulation deliberately **destroys ischaemic peripheral retina** to abolish the VEGF drive — `L19.2` uses it to *\"treat neovascularization\"* after ischaemic vein occlusion, and `L19.1` for proliferative diabetic retinopathy. ⚠️ **In RP it would be actively harmful**: the peripheral retina is exactly the tissue already dying, and burning more of it destroys what peripheral field remains. |\n| **C. Steroids** | Suppress **inflammation**. RP is a genetic dystrophy, not a uveitis. `L20` reserves *\"CORTICOSTEROIDS or IMMUNOSUPPRESSANTS\"* for exudative detachment from uveitis or VKH. |\n| **D. Surgery** | ⚠️ **The subtlest distractor, because one operation genuinely helps.** No surgery treats the dystrophy — but RP causes **complicated cataract** (`L35,36`: *\"Complicated cataract: Uveitis, **Retinal dystrophy, retinitis pigmentosa**, High myopia, Acute glaucoma\"*), and removing that cataract can restore useful central vision in a patient whose macula still works. Grade Gain keys the cataract itself at `opqb-t13-527` and `opqb-t13-542`, and Q27 of this chapter is built on it. So surgery treats a **complication**; it is not *\"the best line of treatment\"* for the disease, and the bank's key is right. |\n\n**What is actually offered a patient like this**, none of it from the course material: genetic counselling and testing, since the inheritance pattern determines the risk to siblings and children; low-vision aids and registration; **cataract surgery** when the posterior subcapsular lens opacity arrives; screening for the syndromic associations (Usher syndrome with sensorineural deafness, Bardet–Biedl); and, for a very small number of patients with confirmed **biallelic RPE65 mutations**, subretinal gene therapy. Vitamin A palmitate has been used on weak evidence. **\"No available treatment\" remains the correct examination answer and the correct clinical summary**, and it is not the same as nothing to offer.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Retinitis pigmentosa is an inherited photoreceptor dystrophy with no treatment that alters its course: anti-VEGF, PRP, steroids and surgery each target a mechanism the disease does not have, and PRP would destroy the very peripheral retina already failing — although cataract surgery for the complicated cataract it causes remains worthwhile.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.88 (Ch.13 Q16; printed as "In the previous case…", antecedent Q15 on the same page)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'A **62-year-old diabetic woman with pre-existing proliferative diabetic retinopathy (PDR)** is at risk of developing which of the following?',
  options:['Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment','Tractional retinal detachment','Exudative retinal detachment','All of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"**The word doing the work is *proliferative*, and `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` attaches it to exactly one type of detachment.** Slide 7:\n\n> **Tractional Retinal Detachment (TRD)** — *\"The retina is **pulled away** from the underlying tissue by **scar tissue or membranes** growing on its surface. **UNLIKE RRD, THERE IS TYPICALLY NO INITIAL TEAR.** Common Causes: **Advanced diabetic retinopathy (THE MOST COMMON CAUSE)**, sickle cell disease, or previous ocular trauma.\"*\n\nand the figure on slide 10 labels that cause **proliferative** diabetic retinopathy, which is what makes this stem's abbreviation the answer rather than the decoration.\n\n**The mechanism is a contraction, not a hole.** Retinal ischaemia in diabetes drives new vessel growth at the disc and along the arcades; those new vessels grow **on the retinal surface and into the posterior vitreous face**, and they carry fibrous tissue with them. Fibrous tissue then does what fibrous tissue does — it shortens. The membrane tents the retina forward at its points of attachment, and the retina comes off with **no break anywhere in it**. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` records the endpoint from the surgical side, listing among the indications for vitreoretinal surgery *\"Progressive proliferation despite laser therapy\"* and ***\"Retinal detachment involving macula\"***.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Rhegmatogenous** | Requires a **break** — *rhegma*, Greek for a tear. `L20` slide 6 gives its causes as *\"Aging (vitreous shrinkage), high myopia, or eye trauma\"*, and diabetes is on none of them. ⚠️ This chapter keys the same distinction from the other side at Q9, where diabetic retinopathy is the **exception** in a list of rhegmatogenous causes. The two keys agree, and reading them together is the fastest way to fix the point. |\n| **C. Exudative** | Fluid leaking under an intact retina from a **choroidal** or inflammatory process — `L20` slide 8: *\"Inflammatory diseases (like uveitis), tumors (such as choroidal melanoma), or systemic conditions like severe hypertension\"*. Diabetes leaks fluid **into** the retina (macular oedema), not **under** it. |\n| **D. All of the above** | Fails on A and C. |\n\n**How to tell them apart at the slit lamp**, from `L20`'s slide 9 table: **Retinal Surface** — *corrugated / wrinkled* (rhegmatogenous) · ***smooth / taut*** (tractional) · *smooth / bullous* (exudative); **Common Risk** — *myopia, aging* · ***diabetes*** · *inflammation, tumours*. A taut, concave, tented retina with visible membranes and no break, in a diabetic, is tractional until proved otherwise.\n\n**And the treatment differs accordingly**, which is why the distinction is not academic. Slide 15: *\"**Vitrectomy & Membrane Peeling** — Surgeons remove the vitreous and carefully **peel away fibrovascular membranes** or scar tissue that is pulling on the retina\"*, plus *\"Underlying Disease Control ... **MOST COMMONLY DIABETIC RETINOPATHY**\"*. A scleral buckle or a gas bubble — the rhegmatogenous operations — would do nothing about a membrane. Grade Gain keys the same fact at `opqb-t13-526`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Proliferative diabetic retinopathy causes tractional retinal detachment: fibrovascular membranes on the retinal surface contract and lift the retina with no break at all, which is why it is the commonest cause of the tractional type and never of the rhegmatogenous one.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.88 (Ch.13 Q17)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
  stem:'A **25-year-old man** complains of **night blindness and decreased peripheral vision**. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['CRAO','BRAO','Retinal detachment','Retinitis pigmentosa'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**Night blindness plus peripheral field loss in a young adult is a rod disease, and the rod disease is retinitis pigmentosa.** The symptom pair is not a coincidence — it is anatomy. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` gives the distribution: the fovea has a *\"high density of cones but no rods\"*, while the macula *\"is composed of both rods and cones\"*. **Rods dominate everywhere outside the centre and they are the receptors that work in dim light.** A disease that kills rods first therefore produces exactly two complaints, in this order: *I cannot see at night*, then *I keep bumping into things* — and central acuity stays normal for years, which is why a 25-year-old presents with this and not with blurred reading.\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.185 files retinitis pigmentosa under **chronic (weeks to months) posterior-segment** visual loss, and p.194 uses it as the worked example of **consecutive optic atrophy** — *\"Which occurs due to retinal diseases e.g., **Retinitis pigmentosa** / degenerative myopia\"*. Both entries say the same thing about tempo: this is a disease measured in years.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and the tempo alone eliminates all three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. CRAO** | **Sudden, painless, severe, monocular, and catastrophic within minutes** — `L19.2)` gives *\"VA < 6/60 · APD marked · retinal whitening · 'cherry-red spot' at macula · very poor prognosis\"*. It is a stroke of the retina; it produces no night blindness, and its field loss is total, not peripheral. `L19.2` also makes it a disease of atherosclerosis and emboli, i.e. of older patients. |\n| **B. BRAO** | The same event confined to one branch, so the field defect is **altitudinal or sectoral and sudden**, arriving in one instant and never progressing. `L19.2)` gives it a *\"very good prognosis in 80 %\"*. Nothing about it is nocturnal. |\n| **C. Retinal detachment** | ⚠️ **The closest distractor, because a detachment genuinely causes peripheral field loss** — `L20)` describes *\"a 'curtain' or shadow in the peripheral vision\"*. But the onset is *\"often sudden and rapid\"*, it is accompanied by **flashes and floaters**, it is **monocular**, and it does not cause night blindness. The discriminator is **night blindness**, which belongs to the rods and to no detachment. |\n\n**⚠️ Two questions in this chapter describe this disease and neither is a duplicate of the other.** Q15 gives the fundus **triad** and asks for the diagnosis; this one gives the **symptoms**. Q3 gives two of the three fundus signs and asks which **test** confirms it — keying electrophysiological tests, since the ERG measures the outer retina directly and `opqb-t13-542` notes it *\"can detect early cases\"*. The discriminating tokens are *bone spicules* (Q15 only) and *tests* against *diagnosis* (Q3 against Q18); the option menus differ in two of four entries.\n\n**⚠️ Note on the printing:** this question's stem and all four options sit on book p.88, but the line *\"Answer: D\"* stands alone at the head of p.89. Both pages were read before the key was recorded.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Night blindness followed by peripheral field loss in a young adult is retinitis pigmentosa, because rods dominate the peripheral retina and work in dim light — so a rod dystrophy takes the night and the periphery first and leaves central acuity intact for years.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.88-89 (Ch.13 Q18; stem and options on p.88, the answer line alone at the head of p.89)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
  stem:'Which of the following is the most common cause of **CRVO / BRVO**?',
  options:['Embolism','Thrombosis','Arteritis','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"**Veins thrombose; arteries embolise. That single sentence answers this question and the next one, and the two are printed side by side with the same four options and different keys.**\n\n**Why a retinal vein clots where it does.** The central retinal artery and vein leave the eye together through the **lamina cribrosa**, wrapped in a common adventitial sheath, and a branch retinal vein crosses its artery at an arteriovenous crossing where the two again share adventitia. When the artery is thickened by hypertension and arteriolosclerosis, it **compresses the vein against that shared sheath**; the lumen narrows, flow becomes turbulent and slow, the endothelium is damaged, and a thrombus forms at exactly that point. This is Virchow's triad applied to one crossing — stasis, endothelial injury and, in the predisposed, hypercoagulability. It is also why branch occlusions happen **at crossings** and not randomly along a vein. *(The anatomy of the shared adventitial sheath is **not taken from the course material**; the deck gives the risk factors and the signs but not the mechanism.)*\n\n**The deck's risk list is a thrombosis list.** `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders`: *\"increasing age · hypertension · diabetes · **coagulation abnormalities** · **raised intraocular pressure** · periphlebitis\"*. Every entry either damages the vessel wall, thickens the blood or squeezes the vein — raised IOP compresses the vein at the disc, which is why the deck lists it, and why Grade Gain keys open-angle glaucoma as a CRVO predisposing factor at `opqb-t13-578`.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Embolism** | ⚠️ **The whole point of the pairing.** An embolus travels **downstream in an artery** and lodges where the lumen narrows. The retinal veins carry blood **away** from the eye toward the cavernous sinus, so an embolus cannot arrive in one from the circulation. `L19.2` reserves emboli entirely for the artery — *\"Types of emboli: Cardiac, Carotid ... Cholesterol emboli (Hollenhorst plaques) ... Fibrinoplatelet emboli ... Calcific emboli\"* — and this bank keys embolism for **CRAO / BRAO** in the very next question. |\n| **C. Arteritis** | Inflammation of an **artery** wall. `L19.2` files *periarteritis* under the causes of arterial vaso-obliteration and notes *\"SLE may cause branch artery occlusion\"*. The venous counterpart exists — **periphlebitis**, which is on the vein risk list — but it is uncommon, and \"arteritis\" is not it. Giant cell arteritis threatens the artery and the optic nerve head, not the vein. |\n| **D. None of the above** | Only if B were absent. |\n\n**⚠️ This question and Q20 print identical option menus in identical order with different keys.** They are two questions, not one: the discriminating token is **\"CRVO / BRVO\"** against **\"CRAO / BRAO\"** — a single letter, V against A, vein against artery. Anyone who merges them deletes an occlusion and leaves the survivor keyed for the wrong vessel.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Retinal vein occlusion is thrombotic: a sclerotic artery compresses the vein inside their shared adventitial sheath at the lamina cribrosa or at an arteriovenous crossing, and a clot forms there — which is why the risk factors are hypertension, diabetes, raised IOP and coagulation abnormalities, and why emboli play no part.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.89 (Ch.13 Q19)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
  stem:'Which of the following is the most common cause of **CRAO / BRAO**?',
  options:['Embolism','Thrombosis','Arteritis','None of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"**`L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` opens its arterial section with a slide headed \"Causes of retinal artery occlusion\" listing exactly two, and embolism is printed first:**\n\n> *\"**Embolism** ... **Vaso-obliteration**\"*\n\nand then devotes three further slides to the emboli themselves — more space than to anything else in the section:\n\n> *\"**Types of emboli: Cardiac · Carotid**\"*\n> **Cholesterol emboli (Hollenhorst plaques)** — *\"Multiple, bright, refractile crystals · often located at arteriolar bifurcations · frequently asymptomatic\"*\n> **Fibrinoplatelet emboli** — *\"Multiple, dull grey particles · occasionally fill entire lumen · **may cause amaurosis fugax** and occasionally permanent obstruction\"*\n> **Calcific emboli** — *\"Usually single, white and close to disc · may cause permanent obstruction\"*\n\nThat emphasis is clinical, not editorial: an embolus in the retina means an embolic **source**, and the deck follows it with four slides on carotid imaging — duplex scanning, digital subtraction angiography, MR angiography, intra-arterial angiography — and then *\"Treatment options for carotid disease\"*. The retina is where a carotid or cardiac lesion becomes visible.\n\n**⚠️ DECK-vs-BANK-KEY DIVERGENCE — recorded, and the key is not disputed.** The same deck, on its vaso-obliteration slide, prints under **atherosclerosis**: ***\"Most common cause of central artery occlusion\"***. Atherosclerotic vaso-obliteration is in-situ occlusion of the vessel at the lamina cribrosa, which is closer to **thrombosis** (option B) than to embolism. On a strict reading of that one caption the deck supports B for the **central** artery, while the bank keys **A** for a stem that lumps central and branch together. Three things make the printed key defensible: *branch* artery occlusion is almost always embolic, and the stem asks about *CRAO / BRAO* jointly; the deck lists embolism first among the causes and gives it four slides to atherosclerosis's one line; and the caption itself sits under *vaso-obliteration*, not under an option offered here — \"atherosclerosis\" is not on the menu, and it is not a synonym for the bare word \"thrombosis\". **Answer A as printed, and know the caption.**\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **B. Thrombosis** | The **venous** mechanism, keyed one question earlier for CRVO / BRVO. See the divergence above for the one caption that complicates this. |\n| **C. Arteritis** | ⚠️ Uncommon but **the one you must not miss**: giant cell arteritis causes arteritic retinal artery occlusion and anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy, and untreated it takes the second eye within days. `L19.2` lists *periarteritis* under vaso-obliteration and notes *\"SLE may cause branch artery occlusion\"*, and its arterial section adds that *\"haematological disorders may cause recurrent occlusions in young individuals — protein S deficiency, protein C deficiency, antithrombin III deficiency, 'sticky platelet syndromes' and antiphospholipid antibody syndrome\"*. Rare causes, and each demands its own test. |\n| **D. None of the above** | Only if A were absent. |\n\n**⚠️ Identical option menu to Q19, in the same order, with a different key.** The discriminating token is **\"CRAO / BRAO\"** against **\"CRVO / BRVO\"**. Two questions.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A retinal artery occlusion is an embolic event until proved otherwise, so the eye is only half the consultation: carotid duplex, cardiac assessment, and — in anyone over fifty — an immediate ESR and CRP for giant cell arteritis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Retinal artery occlusion is usually embolic, from a carotid or cardiac source — cholesterol, fibrinoplatelet or calcific — which is why the finding obliges a search for the source; arteritis is the rare cause that must not be missed, and thrombosis belongs to the veins.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.89 (Ch.13 Q20; same option menu as Q19, different key)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-intro',
  stem:'Which of the following is **true** regarding the retina?',
  options:['Macula is the most sensitive area of the retina','Fovea is responsible for detailed fine vision','Rods are highly concentrated in paracentral and peripheral retina','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**All three statements are printed, in substance, on one slide of `L35,36) Chronic visual loss`:**\n\n> *\"The **macula** is composed of **both rods and cones** and is the area responsible for **detailed, fine central vision**. The **fovea** ... has a **high density of cones but no rods**.\"*\n\n**A — the macula is the most sensitive area of the retina.** \"Sensitive\" here means **visual sensitivity**: acuity and discrimination, not light detection. The macula is the only part of the retina that resolves fine detail, which is why a lesion a millimetre across there costs more vision than a lesion ten times larger in the periphery. It is also why the macular symptom list is so distinctive — `L35,36` gives *\"diminution of vision · colour defects · micropsia · macropsia · metamorphopsia · scotoma\"*.\n\n**B — the fovea is responsible for detailed fine vision.** The deck's own phrase, and Grade Gain keys the anatomy directly at `opqb-t1-14`: the fovea has the **highest concentration of cones**. Cones are packed there at maximum density, each with a near-private line to the brain, and the inner retinal layers are swept aside so light reaches them unobstructed. `opqb-t13-546` adds that the fovea is nourished by the **choriocapillaris** — the reason it stays transparent in an arterial occlusion and shows as the cherry-red spot of Q10 and Q26.\n\n**C — rods are highly concentrated in the paracentral and peripheral retina.** The complement of B. The fovea has *\"no rods\"* at all; rod density peaks a few millimetres out and stays high to the periphery. This is the anatomy behind the whole of retinitis pigmentosa in this chapter: a rod disease takes **night vision and peripheral field** first (Q18) and reaches the centre last.\n\n**⚠️ A and B look as if they contradict each other, and that is the trap in this question.** If the fovea is responsible for fine vision, how is the macula the most sensitive area? Because **the fovea is part of the macula** — it is its centre. The statements are nested, not competing. A candidate who reads them as mutually exclusive will pick one and lose the item. *(Strictly, the peak of visual sensitivity is the **foveola** rather than the macula as a whole, so statement A is true at the level of precision the deck uses and loose at a finer one — **not taken from the course material**. The bank keys \"All of the above\", and every statement is true as the module teaches it.)*\n\n**Why E is wrong:** all three statements are supported, so *None of the above* fails three times over.\n\n**How to hold the anatomy.** Two receptor systems with opposite jobs and opposite distributions: **cones** — central, colour, fine detail, bright light, densest at the fovea; **rods** — peripheral, monochrome, motion and dim light, absent from the fovea. Nearly every retinal disease in this chapter can be predicted from which system it attacks first.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'The fovea is the cone-packed centre of the macula and gives detailed fine vision, the macula is the retina\'s area of greatest visual sensitivity, and rods — absent from the fovea — are concentrated paracentrally and peripherally, which is why rod disease costs night and peripheral vision first.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.89 (Ch.13 Q21)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
  stem:'Which of the following is a **simple test** that can be used to **monitor AMD**?',
  options:['Fluorescein angiography','Amsler grid','OCT','Ophthalmoscopy'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"**The Amsler grid is a square of graph paper with a central dot, and it is the only test on this list a patient can do alone, at home, every morning.** That is what *simple* and *monitor* are asking for. `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` lists it among the *\"Test for macular function\"*: *\"V/A · Pupillary light reaction · Colour vision · Ophthalmoscopy · **Amsilar grid** [sic] · Photostress test · Laser interferometry · Fluorescein angiography\"*.\n\n**Why a grid detects this disease and nothing else does as early.** The patient covers one eye, fixes the central dot at reading distance and reports any line that is **wavy, broken or missing**. That is **metamorphopsia** and **scotoma** — two entries on the deck's own macular symptom list — and in wet AMD they appear when a choroidal neovascular membrane first lifts and distorts the photoreceptor sheet. Because `L35,36` treats wet AMD with *\"injections of Anti VEGF\"* and the benefit depends on starting before the fovea scars, a test the patient performs daily converts a hospital diagnosis into a same-week one. The grid finds nothing; it tells the patient **when to come**.\n\n*(⚠️ **Two printing faults in this question, both verified by re-rendering the page at 2.5×.** Option A prints **\"Flourescine angiography\"** — but that is **the deck's own misspelling**, printed identically in `L35,36`'s macular-function list, so it is inherited rather than introduced. Option C prints **\"C.OCT\"** with no space after the option letter; the option text is *OCT*. Both are corrected above and recorded here. ⚠️ Note the reversal: `L35,36` misspells the grid as **\"Amsilar grid\"** and this bank spells **Amsler** correctly — the bank is right where the deck is wrong. Measured: *Amsler grid* appears in **six** of the twenty-nine cached decks and only `L35,36` misspells it; `L1,2)`, `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination`, `L21) Optic Nerve` and `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` all spell it correctly, so a grep on the correct spelling finds the test easily and a grep on the deck's spelling finds only this one slide.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and none of them is a bad test, only the wrong kind of test:**\n\n| Option | What it is good for | Why not here |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Fluorescein angiography** | **Diagnosis** of the neovascular membrane — it shows leakage and defines the lesion, and `L35,36` lists it among the macular-function tests | Invasive: an intravenous dye, a photographic sequence, a hospital visit, and a real if small risk of anaphylaxis. Nobody does it monthly to *monitor*, and the word *simple* excludes it outright |\n| **C. OCT** | The modern standard for **following** wet AMD — cross-sectional retinal imaging that measures fluid to the micron and decides each anti-VEGF injection | ⚠️ **The strongest distractor, and it is genuinely how AMD is monitored in a clinic.** But it needs a machine, a clinic and an operator. Against *simple*, the grid wins; the two are complementary, and in practice the patient uses the grid at home and the OCT is done at each visit |\n| **D. Ophthalmoscopy** | Seeing the **drusen**, the pigment changes, the haemorrhage — the examination that made the diagnosis in Q1 and Q5 | Requires an examiner, and it shows structure rather than function. A patient's central vision can distort before the fundus visibly changes |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Give every patient with drusen an Amsler grid and one instruction: *test each eye separately, every day, and come the same week if a line bends*. In wet AMD the interval between distortion and irreversible foveal scarring is measured in weeks.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'The Amsler grid is the simple test for monitoring macular degeneration: the patient uses it alone at home, it detects metamorphopsia and scotoma as they appear, and it exists to tell the patient when to seek anti-VEGF treatment — whereas angiography and OCT diagnose and quantify but need a hospital.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.89 (Ch.13 Q22; option A printed "Flourescine angiography", option C printed "C.OCT")' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **leading cause of irreversible blindness in the western world**?',
  options:['Diabetic retinopathy','Hypertensive retinopathy','AMD','CRVO','None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**The bank keys age-related macular degeneration, and `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` supports it on the macular-degeneration slide:**\n\n> *\"In the United States, **age-related macular degeneration is the leading cause of irreversible central visual loss (20/200 or worse) among people aged 52 or older**.\"*\n\nThe reasoning behind the claim is demographic. AMD is a disease of the old; western populations are old and getting older; and the loss is **central**, which is the vision that ends reading, driving and recognising faces — so it is counted as blindness even when the peripheral field is untouched. The **dry** form (`L35,36`: *\"90 % slow progressive atrophy of RPE and photoreceptors\"*) has no treatment beyond *\"dietary supplements of minerals and vitamins especially Vit. E\"*, and geographic atrophy of the fovea is irreversible.\n\n**⚠️ DECK-vs-BANK-KEY DIVERGENCE — recorded, and the key is not disputed.** The **same deck**, on its **glaucoma** slide, prints ***\"Leading cause of irreversible blindness\"*** — flatly, with no qualifier. Two slides in one lecture award the title to two different diseases. The difference is in the words the bank's stem drops:\n\n| | What the deck actually claims | What the stem asks |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Glaucoma slide** | *\"Leading cause of **irreversible blindness**\"* — unqualified, and generally meant **worldwide** | *\"leading cause of irreversible blindness in the western world\"* |\n| **AMD slide** | Leading cause of irreversible **CENTRAL** visual loss, **20/200 or worse**, **aged 52 or older**, **in the United States** | — |\n\nThe stem keeps *irreversible* and *blindness*, keeps a western geography, and drops **central** and the age qualifier. Read strictly against the glaucoma slide the answer would be glaucoma, which is not on the option list at all; read against the AMD slide with its qualifiers restored, AMD is right. **Since glaucoma is not offered, AMD is the best available answer and the printed key stands.** The two claims are reconciled in practice by their scope: glaucoma leads worldwide and for irreversible blindness overall, AMD leads for irreversible **central** loss in elderly western populations. *(That reconciliation is **not taken from the course material** — the deck prints both claims and never sets them against each other.)*\n\n**⚠️ And note a third claim in the same lecture, which is not in competition:** senile cataract is *\"the primary cause of **reversible** blindness of Egypt\"*. Reversible against irreversible is the axis that keeps all three statements true at once, and it is the single most useful distinction in this topic.\n\n**Why the other four are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Diabetic retinopathy** | ⚠️ The strongest distractor, and it holds a real title: the leading cause of blindness in the **working-age** population. But it is **largely preventable and treatable** — laser, anti-VEGF, vitrectomy — so much of its damage is not irreversible, and it does not lead overall. |\n| **B. Hypertensive retinopathy** | Rarely blinds by itself. `L19.2` teaches it as a **grading** exercise with *\"flame-shaped retinal haemorrhages · cotton-wool spots and macular star · disc oedema\"*, and its real importance is as a marker of systemic disease and a risk factor for the occlusions in this chapter. Malignant hypertension can cause exudative detachment (`L20`), which typically resolves when the pressure is controlled. |\n| **D. CRVO** | A common and serious **individual** event, but it is one eye at a time and it is not a population-level leading cause. `L19.2` gives the non-ischaemic form a *\"guarded prognosis\"* and the ischaemic form a *\"very poor\"* one. |\n| **E. None of the above** | Only if C were absent. |\n\n**⚠️ Note on the printing:** the stem and all five options are on book p.89; the line *\"Answer: C\"* stands alone at the head of p.90. Both were read before the key was recorded.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Age-related macular degeneration is the leading cause of irreversible central visual loss in elderly western populations, because it is untreatable in its dry form and destroys exactly the vision that matters — noting that the same lecture separately calls glaucoma the leading cause of irreversible blindness, unqualified.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.89-90 (Ch.13 Q23; stem and options on p.89, the answer line alone at the head of p.90)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
  stem:'Which of the following is **NOT** among the differential diagnosis of **cherry red spot** seen in ophthalmoscopy?',
  options:['CRAO / BRAO','Penetrating eye trauma','Niemann-Pick disease','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"**A cherry-red spot is not a lesion — it is a contrast effect, and it requires the retina AROUND the fovea to turn white.** The fovea has no inner retinal layers and is fed by the choriocapillaris (`opqb-t13-546`), so the orange choroid always shows through it. Whiten everything else and that untouched centre stands out as a red dot. **Anything that makes the perifoveal retina opaque produces the sign; anything that does not, cannot.**\n\n**Why penetrating trauma is the exception.** A penetrating injury makes a **hole** — it lacerates the coats at one place. Its consequences are a distorted globe, hypotony, vitreous haemorrhage, endophthalmitis, retinal incarceration, and later sympathetic ophthalmia. None of those whitens the retina uniformly around the macula. **Blunt trauma is a different matter entirely, and this is where the question is sharp:** concussive injury produces **commotio retinae**, and `L25) Trauma I` labels its fundus photograph ***\"CHERRY-RED SPOT · RETINAL EDEMA\"***. Grade Gain keys commotio retinae as a genuine cause of the sign at `opqb-t18-743`. So the discriminator is not *trauma* — it is **penetrating against blunt**, and the bank has chosen its adjective carefully.\n\n**Why the other options do belong on the differential:**\n\n| Option | How it whitens the perifoveal retina |\n|---|---|\n| **A. CRAO / BRAO** | **Infarction** of the inner retina. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints *\"Retinal whitening · 'Cherry-red spot' at macula\"* on the CRAO slide, and `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` gives its Case 1 as *\"a white, opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot\"*. ⚠️ **Option A is imperfect and it is recorded here:** a **branch** artery occlusion whitens only the sector it supplied, so it classically produces a **sectoral** area of whitening and a cherry-red spot only if the branch happens to supply the perifoveal retina. Pairing BRAO with CRAO overstates the case. Since the option is not the key, this does not affect the answer. |\n| **C. Niemann-Pick disease** | A **lysosomal storage** disease. Sphingomyelin accumulates inside retinal ganglion cells, and where those cells are stacked many layers deep — in a ring around the fovea — the swollen white cytoplasm turns the retina opaque. The fovea, which has no ganglion cells at all, stays clear. It is the same mechanism as **Tay–Sachs**, which Grade Gain lists explicitly at `opqb-t18-743`. |\n| **D. None of the above** | Fails, because B is the exception. |\n\n*(⚠️ **Two faults recorded.** The bank prints option C as **\"Niemann-pick disease\"**, lower-case *p* in *Pick*, verified at 2.5×; corrected above. And **\"niemann\" returns ZERO hits across all twenty-nine cached ophthalmology lecture files and all twelve cached book chapters** — the disease is asked about and never taught. The storage mechanism above is therefore **not taken from the course material**, though the module does supply the parallel case: `opqb-t18-743` keys Tay–Sachs, the same class of disease by the same mechanism.)*\n\n**How to run this differential in an exam.** Ask what could have whitened the retina around the fovea, and there are only three answers: **it infarcted** (arterial occlusion), **it was concussed** (commotio retinae, from blunt trauma), or **it is full of stored material** (the sphingolipidoses — Tay–Sachs, Niemann–Pick, and the other gangliosidoses). Everything else on any option list is a distractor.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'A cherry-red spot appears whenever the perifoveal retina turns white while the inner-layer-free fovea stays clear — from arterial infarction, from concussive commotio retinae after blunt trauma, or from ganglion-cell storage disease; a penetrating injury lacerates rather than whitens, so it alone is not on the differential.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.90 (Ch.13 Q24; option C printed "Niemann-pick disease")' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
  stem:'A **45-year-old male** patient was diagnosed with **complete central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO)**. However, **vision was partially intact**. Which of the following is the cause for that partial preservation of vision in that patient?',
  options:['Development of collaterals that bypass the occluded segment','Cilioretinal artery','Lucid interval with terminal loss of vision','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"**A cilioretinal artery is an anatomical variant that enters the retina from the wrong circulation, so an occlusion of the central retinal artery never reaches it.** `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders`:\n\n> **Cilioretinal artery** — *\"derived from the **posterior ciliary circulation**, present in about **30 % of individuals**\"*\n\nThat single fact answers the question. The central retinal artery and the posterior ciliary arteries are separate branches of the ophthalmic artery, dividing **before** either reaches the eye. A clot lodged in the central retinal artery at the lamina cribrosa is therefore upstream of nothing in the ciliary system. Where a cilioretinal artery exists and happens to supply the papillomacular bundle — as it usually does — that wedge of retina keeps its blood, stays pink while everything around it whitens, and **keeps seeing**. The patient's central acuity can be near normal in an eye whose entire remaining retina has infarcted.\n\n`L19.2` shows it directly on the angiogram: its CRAO fluorescein sequence is captioned ***\"Early filling of cilioretinal artery · Non-filling of other vessels · Late staining of vessel walls\"*** — one vessel filling normally in a dead circulation. Grade Gain keys the same variant as the cause of central-field sparing at `opqb-t13-516`, and `opqb-t13-566`'s explanation notes that an eye with one *\"keeps a central island\"*.\n\n*(⚠️ Cilioretinal **sparing** as a named phenomenon is on `L31,32) Acute visual Loss`'s explicit not-covered list; the deck teaches the artery's existence, origin and prevalence, and this bank supplies the consequence. The reciprocal event — **cilioretinal artery occlusion**, which takes the central vision and leaves the rest — has its own slide in `L19.2`.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Development of collaterals that bypass the occluded segment** | ⚠️ **The trap, and it fails on timing and on anatomy.** The retinal arterial circulation is functionally **end-arterial**: retinal arterioles do not anastomose with one another, which is precisely why a single embolus infarcts everything downstream. And collaterals, where they form at all, take weeks to months — this vision was preserved from the first moment. Where the module does teach collaterals it is on the **venous** side: `L19.2`'s slide on old branch vein occlusion shows *\"vascular sheathing and **collaterals**\"* developing after the event. Right word, wrong vessel and wrong clock. |\n| **C. Lucid interval with terminal loss of vision** | A borrowed neurosurgical phrase — the lucid interval of an extradural haematoma. Retinal infarction has no lucid interval; the loss is immediate and, as `L19.2` puts it, the prognosis is *\"very poor\"*. Nothing in the module describes a delayed pattern of loss in CRAO. |\n| **D. None of the above** | Only if B were absent. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Preserved central vision does **not** argue against a diagnosis of complete central retinal artery occlusion — in roughly a third of people it is exactly what a cilioretinal artery predicts. The eye still needs the same urgent work-up: carotid duplex, cardiac source, and ESR and CRP for giant cell arteritis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'A cilioretinal artery arises from the posterior ciliary circulation rather than the central retinal artery and is present in about 30% of people, so it survives a complete CRAO and keeps its wedge of macula perfused — the retinal circulation is otherwise end-arterial and forms no rescuing collaterals.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.90 (Ch.13 Q25)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
  stem:'A **70-year-old man** presents with **sudden, painless loss of vision** in his right eye. Fundus exam shows a **pale retina with a bright cherry-red spot at the fovea**. A **marked relative afferent pupillary defect** is present in the right eye. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Central retinal vein occlusion','Optic neuritis','Central retinal artery occlusion','Branch retinal artery occlusion'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**Three findings, and `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints all three on one slide:**\n\n> **Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO)** — *\"**VA < 6/60** · **APD - marked** · **Retinal whitening** · **'Cherry-red spot' at macula** · Arteriolar and venular narrowing · Sludging and segmentation of blood column (cattle-trucking) · **Very poor prognosis**\"*\n\n`L31,32) Acute visual Loss` builds its Case 1 on the same patient — a 70-year-old with *\"a white, opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot\"*, diagnosis *\"Central Retinal Artery Occlusion\"*, and it also gives the pupil in words: *\"The right pupil does not respond to light directly but does react consensually ... The swinging-flashlight test confirms a right **relative afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil)**.\"*\n\n**Why the RAPD is the finding that clinches it, and what it means.** The lesion is **afferent** — the retina cannot transmit a signal — while the efferent pathway is untouched. So light into the bad eye constricts neither pupil, and light into the good eye constricts both, including the bad one. On the swinging-flashlight test the affected pupil therefore appears to *dilate* as the light comes to it. A **marked** RAPD means most of the retinal or optic nerve input is gone, which fits a complete arterial occlusion exactly.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. CRVO** | Also sudden and painless, and it can carry an RAPD — `L19.2` gives *\"APD - marked\"* on the **ischaemic** CRVO slide too. **The fundus is what separates them.** A vein occlusion is *\"marked venous tortuosity and engorgement · extensive retinal haemorrhages · severe disc oedema\"* — this bank's own \"blood and thunder\" at Q12. **No cherry-red spot**, and Grade Gain keys precisely that at `opqb-t13-514`, where every listed CRVO feature is right except *cherry red spot*. Red fundus against pale fundus. |\n| **B. Optic neuritis** | ⚠️ **The RAPD's other home**, and the reason the pupil alone cannot decide this. Optic neuritis produces a marked RAPD — but it is a disease of **young adults**, it is characteristically **painful on eye movement**, and its fundus is either normal (retrobulbar: \"the patient sees nothing and the doctor sees nothing\") or shows a **swollen** disc. This man is 70, his loss is painless, and his retina is pale with a cherry-red spot. Nothing fits. |\n| **D. BRAO** | The right mechanism in the wrong territory. A branch occlusion whitens **one sector**, so the field defect is **altitudinal or sectoral** rather than total, the RAPD is mild or absent, and `L19.2` gives it a *\"very good prognosis in 80 %\"*. The stem describes a **pale retina** — the whole of it — with a cherry-red spot, which requires the *central* artery. |\n\n**⚠️ Not a fold with Q10 of this chapter**, which is also sudden painless monocular loss keyed to CRAO. Q10 prints *\"venous stasis\"* and offers five options (CRAO / CRVO / retinal detachment / AMD / retinitis pigmentosa, key A); this one prints *\"relative afferent pupillary defect\"* and offers four (CRVO / optic neuritis / CRAO / BRAO, key C), and spells the diagnosis out where Q10 abbreviates it. The menus share two entries and the key letters differ. **Two questions.**\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In anyone over fifty with this picture, the questions that matter are not about the eye, which is largely lost: **ESR and CRP the same hour** for giant cell arteritis, then carotid duplex and cardiac assessment for the embolic source. `L19.2` gives four slides to carotid imaging and none to treating the retina.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Sudden painless monocular loss in an elderly patient with a pale retina, a cherry-red spot and a marked relative afferent pupillary defect is central retinal artery occlusion — the RAPD marks the afferent lesion, and the pale fundus separates it from the haemorrhagic fundus of vein occlusion and the normal or swollen disc of optic neuritis.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.90 (Ch.13 Q26)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c13-27', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
  stem:'Which type of **cataract** is commonly associated with **retinitis pigmentosa**?',
  options:['Nuclear sclerosis','Cortical cataract','Posterior subcapsular cataract','Congenital lamellar cataract'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**The module names the association and stops one step short of the answer, so this question turns on a gap the other bank fills.** `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` lists the causes of complicated cataract:\n\n> **Complicated cataract** — *\"Uveitis · **Retinal dystrophy, retinitis pigmentosa** · High myopia · Acute glaucoma\"*\n\nand classifies cataract morphologically two slides later — *\"1-morphologic: nuclear, subcapsular, cortical\"* — **but never says which morphology a complicated cataract takes.** The link the question asks for is not printed in any cached deck or book chapter.\n\n**Grade Gain prints it, in its own explanation box** at `opqb-t13-527`, listing the clinical features of retinitis pigmentosa: *\"**Glare (decreased visual performance in bright light) from posterior subcapsular cataracts. (Common in longstanding cases)**\"*. That is the bank-to-bank corroboration for this key, and the two banks agree.\n\n**⚠️ BANK-vs-BANK DIVERGENCE — recorded, and no key moves.** Grade Gain's `opqb-t13-542` prints **\"posterior *cortical* cataract\"** and keys that statement TRUE, while its own `opqb-t13-527` box prints **\"posterior *subcapsular*\"**. The corpus already records that internal contradiction. **House keys posterior subcapsular, agreeing with `opqb-t13-527` and diverging from `opqb-t13-542`.** Posterior subcapsular is the correct term; all three entries stay keyed as printed.\n\n**Why the opacity forms where it does.** A complicated cataract is one caused by another intraocular disease rather than by age. The **posterior subcapsular** region is the lens's most vulnerable surface: it is where the lens epithelial cells finish their lifelong migration, it sits directly against the vitreous and so is the first part of the lens exposed to whatever the diseased retina releases, and it has no protective epithelium of its own. Chronic retinal degeneration disturbs that microenvironment and the cells there swell and opacify. *(The mechanism is **not taken from the course material** — the deck names the association without explaining it.)*\n\n**Why the position matters clinically.** A posterior subcapsular opacity sits at the **nodal point of the eye**, directly on the visual axis, so it scatters light far out of proportion to its size. Two symptoms follow, and both are printed by the other bank: **glare** in bright light, and worse vision in sunshine than in dim light, because a constricted pupil is entirely filled by the opacity. Vision can be poor while the lens still looks nearly clear.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually is |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Nuclear sclerosis** | The **age-related** cataract of the lens centre. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 lists it among the normal changes of ageing — *\"yellowing and opacification of the lens nucleus (**nuclear sclerosis**)\"*. It causes gradual dimming and an index myopic shift (\"second sight\"), and it has nothing to do with retinal disease. |\n| **B. Cortical cataract** | Wedge-shaped spoke opacities in the lens cortex, again typically age-related and often associated with diabetes and UV exposure. ⚠️ **The nearest miss**, because `opqb-t13-542` prints *\"posterior cortical\"* — see the divergence above. |\n| **D. Congenital lamellar cataract** | Present from birth or infancy, affecting one lamella of the lens. `L35,36`'s congenital list is a different block entirely — *\"Intrauterine causes: Rubella, toxo, cmv · Syndromes: Down syndrome · Hereditary 1/3\"*. Retinitis pigmentosa declares itself in childhood or adolescence but its cataract is **acquired and late**, in longstanding cases. |\n\n**⚠️ Straddling chapter.** Filed under `op-cat` because what the question tests is a **cataract morphology**; the disease behind it belongs to `op-insid`, where Q3, Q15, Q16 and Q18 of this chapter sit.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** This is the one treatable thing in retinitis pigmentosa. A patient whose vision has dropped faster than the dystrophy explains, and who complains of glare, should have the lens examined — cataract extraction can restore useful central vision for as long as the macula survives.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Retinitis pigmentosa causes a complicated cataract of the posterior subcapsular type — an opacity on the visual axis at the nodal point, which produces glare and worse vision in bright light than dim, and which is the one part of the disease that surgery can still help.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.90 (Ch.13 Q27, the last question of the chapter; the lower third of the page is blank)' }
